 "title": "The moment we stopped understanding AI [AlexNet]",
 "text_head": "Welch Labs traces how AlexNet's 2012 ImageNet win replaced hand-engineered vision pipelines like SIFT with learned convolutional features, then uses interpretability tools \u2014 first-layer kernel images, activation maps, feature visualization, nearest neighbors in the 4,096-dimensional penultimate layer, and Activation Atlases \u2014 to argue that the same scaled-up \"dumb compute block\" architecture that produced ChatGPT is precisely what made these models opaque.\n\nAlexNet is walked through layer by layer: its first convolutional layer holds 96 kernels of size 11\u00d711\u00d73, small enough to render directly ",
